CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Forests in India are classified by the Forest Department into three categories. More than half of the total forest land has been declared reserved forests, which are regarded as the most valuable for the conservation of forest and wildlife. Almost one-third of the total forest area is protected forest, which is protected from further depletion. The remaining forests and wastelands belong to both government and private individuals and communities. "
   r"(i) Name the three categories of forests. (ii) Which category is regarded as the most valuable for conservation? (iii) Which category forms almost one-third of the forest area? (iv) Why is it important to protect forests as well as wildlife?",
   r"(i) Reserved, protected and unclassed forests (1). (ii) Reserved forests (1). (iii) Protected forests (1). (iv) Forests and wildlife maintain ecological balance, keep air, water and soil healthy, and preserve biodiversity and genetic diversity on which agriculture and fisheries depend (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In the 1980s, a village in Odisha formed a local committee to protect a degraded forest managed by the Forest Department. Odisha had passed the first resolution for joint forest management in 1988. In return for protection, the villagers are entitled to benefits such as non-timber forest products and a share in the timber from successful protection. "
   r"(i) Which state passed the first resolution on JFM? (ii) Who undertakes the protection work under JFM? (iii) Give any one benefit the community gets. (iv) How is JFM better than a policy in which only the Forest Department protects forests?",
   r"(i) Odisha (1). (ii) Local (village) institutions or committees (1). (iii) Non-timber forest products or a share in the timber (1). (iv) It involves local communities who depend on the forest, so they protect it, restore degraded land and check illegal felling, which reduces the burden on the Department (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In the Himalayas, villagers in Uttarakhand hugged trees to stop contractors from felling them. The Chipko movement showed that communities could resist deforestation and that community afforestation with indigenous species can be successful. Meanwhile, the tiger population in India fell sharply, and Project Tiger was launched in 1973 to protect it. "
   r"(i) Name the movement in which people hugged trees. (ii) In which year was Project Tiger launched? (iii) Name one threat to tigers. (iv) Why do sacred groves also help conservation?",
   r"(i) The Chipko movement (1). (ii) 1973 (1). (iii) Poaching or trade in tiger skins and bones, shrinking habitat (any one) (1). (iv) Local belief treats them as forests of gods and goddesses, so they are left untouched and preserve rare species and old forest patches (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

